title: osquery and Velociraptor, hands-on
guide: howto/osquery-and-velociraptor.html
promise: Question a suspect Linux virtual machine (VM) with osquery's SQL (Structured Query Language) and Velociraptor's VQL (Velociraptor Query Language), pack the answers into evidence, and learn why a query comes back empty when it cannot see what is there.

== Who it is for and what you need
section: How to use this guide
It is for intermediate readers at home on the Linux command line who can read a SQL query that joins two tables. Allow about two hours.

You need Docker and a terminal, but no account and no cloud.

== How the guide was checked
section: How to use this guide
A script ran every code block in order on an Apple silicon Mac, with osquery 5.24.1 and Velociraptor 0.77.3 installed in Ubuntu 24.04 containers.

Not run: anything on a real cloud instance. osquery's systemd_units table was seen only failing.

== How to follow the guide
section: How to use this guide
Read it in order. Paste BASH blocks starting with docker into your machine's terminal, others into a container's shell. There, run osqueryi, osquery's shell, for SQL; .exit leaves it. VQL goes into step 8's vql helper. Unlabelled blocks show output.

== Set up: start the stand-in VM
section: 1. Start the stand-in VM and install osquery
In your machine's terminal, these create a volume (storage Docker can attach to more than one container), start an Ubuntu 24.04 container standing in for the suspect VM and open its root shell. Step 11's last block removes them all.
```bash
docker volume create csoh-osq-dsock
docker run -d --name csoh-osq-vm --hostname web-01 -v csoh-osq-dsock:/run/dind ubuntu:24.04 sleep infinity
docker exec -it csoh-osq-vm bash
```

== Set up: osquery, then plant the compromise
section: 1. Start the stand-in VM and install osquery
Inside the VM, these install osquery from its GitHub release; compare the hash with the release page's digest. Expect osqueryi version 5.24.1. Then paste step 2's block, the attacker's work, in the VM too; it makes no network connection.
```bash
apt-get update -qq && apt-get install -y -qq curl ca-certificates > /dev/null
ARCH=$(dpkg --print-architecture)
curl -fsSLO "https://github.com/osquery/osquery/releases/download/5.24.1/osquery_5.24.1-1.linux_${ARCH}.deb"
sha256sum osquery_5.24.1-1.linux_${ARCH}.deb
dpkg -i osquery_5.24.1-1.linux_${ARCH}.deb > /dev/null
osqueryi --version
```

== What osquery and Velociraptor are
section: Two ways to question a running machine
osquery presents an operating system as tables you read with SQL. Left running across fleets, it may be on a cloud VM already.

Velociraptor, built for incident response, is one binary: copy it across and collect evidence into a zip.

== A table reports only what it could see
section: A table reports only what it could see
A table is a function that runs when you query it. Unable to see something, it returns fewer rows or none, without failing. Every query you rely on needs a control: something you know is there, found the same way.

== Step 3: tie each listening port to its process
section: 3. What is running, and what is listening
osquery's processes table lists kworkerd, named like a kernel worker, run from a hidden directory; its on_disk column is 0: the binary is deleted. Joining listening_ports on pid adds its command line: a one-line Perl server on port 4444.
```sql
SELECT l.port, l.address, p.pid, p.name, p.cmdline
FROM listening_ports l JOIN processes p USING (pid);
```

== Step 4: a per-user table reads only one account
section: 4. Who can log in, and who is asking
Queried alone, osquery's authorized_keys table of SSH (Secure Shell) keys lists only root's, with a warning: given no uid, this per-user table reads only the querying account's home. Joined to the users table, it finds svc-backup's, a new sudo account.
```sql
SELECT u.username, k.key_file, k.comment
FROM users u JOIN authorized_keys k USING (uid);
```

== Step 6: no metadata service, a five-minute wait
section: 6. Containers and cloud metadata
osquery's ec2_instance_metadata table reads the metadata service an AWS (Amazon Web Services) VM asks for facts about itself. Nothing answers here: it retries for about five minutes, logs errors, returns a row of empty strings and exits with status 0.
```bash
time osqueryi "SELECT instance_id, region, iam_arn FROM ec2_instance_metadata;"
```

== Step 7: event tables need the daemon
section: 7. Event tables need the daemon
osquery's file_events table is fed by the kernel's file watches. osqueryi runs with events off, so this prints only a warning. The daemon, osqueryd, a background service, records events from when step 7 starts it; step 2's changes never appear.
```sql
SELECT target_path, action FROM file_events;
```

== Step 8: Velociraptor and a first VQL query
section: 8. Install Velociraptor and run a first query
In the VM, step 8 installs Velociraptor and defines a vql helper: type vql, paste a query, press Ctrl-D on an empty line. FROM names a plugin, called like a function. =~ matches regular expressions: kworkerd's path ends with (deleted).
```vql
SELECT Pid, Name, Exe, CommandLine FROM pslist() WHERE Exe =~ "deleted"
```

== Step 9: a wrong accessor or argument is silent
section: 9. LET, foreach and accessors
Velociraptor plugins that open files take an accessor argument, saying how to read paths. With ntfs, an accessor this Linux build lacks, or the globs argument misspelt, the query prints nothing and exits with status 0. Only -v says why.
```bash
velociraptor -v query 'SELECT OSPath FROM glob(globs="/etc/systemd/system/*.service", accessor="ntfs")' 2>&1 | grep 'glob:'
velociraptor -v query 'SELECT OSPath FROM glob(glob="/etc/systemd/system/*.service")' 2>&1 | grep 'glob:'
```

== Step 10: an artifact's defaults leave root out
section: 10. Artifacts: queries with documentation and defaults
A Velociraptor artifact packages VQL queries with a description and parameters with defaults. By default, Linux.Ssh.AuthorizedKeys looks only under /home, missing root's key: step 4's blind spot reversed. This widens its sshKeyFilesGlob parameter to root's home with a brace list.
```vql
SELECT OSPath, count() AS Keys
FROM Artifact.Linux.Ssh.AuthorizedKeys(sshKeyFilesGlob="{/root,/home/*}/.ssh/authorized_keys*")
GROUP BY OSPath
```

== Step 11: read the evidence through its accessor
section: 11. Collect the evidence and hand it on
An offline collector, Velociraptor with built-in artifacts, writes one zip on the VM. Step 11 records its hash and copies it to a workstation container. This query reads it through the collector accessor; with no accessor, it reads the workstation.
```vql
SELECT OSPath.Path AS Path, Size
FROM glob(globs="/uploads/auto/**", root=pathspec(DelegatePath="/root/Collection-web-01.zip"), accessor="collector")
WHERE NOT IsDir
ORDER BY Path
```

== Exercise: what is the deleted listener?
section: Hands-on exercises
The listener's binary has been deleted. Get its SHA-256 hash from the running process with osquery, and find out what program it is. Do it on the VM before step 11's clean-up. The guide hides answers under "Show the answer".

== Answer: hash the process through /proc
section: Hands-on exercises
The kernel keeps a deleted executable readable through /proc/<pid>/exe while the process runs, and osquery's hash table reads that path. The guide's second query compares the hash with every file in /usr/bin and matches /usr/bin/perl: it is Perl, renamed.
```sql
SELECT p.pid, p.name, h.sha256
FROM processes p JOIN hash h ON h.path = '/proc/' || p.pid || '/exe'
WHERE p.on_disk = 0;
```

== Exercise: what changed in the last day?
section: Hands-on exercises
Write one VQL query that lists every file under /etc/cron.d and /etc/systemd/system, and any .ssh directory in /root or /home, changed in the last day, newest first. Run it through vql on the VM, before step 11's clean-up.

== Answer: one glob() over a list of patterns
section: Hands-on exercises
Velociraptor's glob() plugin walks a list of patterns in one pass; ** matches any depth. The WHERE clause keeps files changed within the last 86,400 seconds. Modification times are under the attacker's control: this finds careless changes, not every change.
```vql
SELECT Mtime, OSPath
FROM glob(globs=["/etc/cron.d/**", "/etc/systemd/system/**", "{/root,/home/*}/.ssh/**"])
WHERE NOT IsDir AND Mtime > now() - 86400
ORDER BY Mtime DESC
```

== Common mistakes
section: Common mistakes
- Without root, osquery silently loses process paths, listener owners and other users' files.
- Outside a lab, encrypt the collection zip: it carries keys and history.
- The tools change the machine: snapshot its disk through the cloud provider first.
